{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CHANGE 2 AUTHORED — the cutover itself, STACKED ON the safety-net change so the ordering criterion is structural rather than procedural.\n\n    07d2ecfe54  eb6e: point the review bot at Bedrock      <- Change 2 (Change-Id I837be12fbe2b3b810947128f2a1680b9ab3e83ad)\n    c8d1685bb1  eb6e: Bedrock safety net and packaging     <- Change 1 (Change-Id I9762105851d88e6e6d02ef68d06c195c91602ad9)\n    b301e7513d  (origin/main)\nChange 2 cannot merge before Change 1 because it is its child. Note this is only the COMMIT ordering; the criterion's substantive half — the safety net PROVEN before the flip TAKES EFFECT — is enforced by me holding submission of Change 2 until Change 1 is submitted, applied, and deployed AND the induced-VOTER_ERROR drill is recorded.\n\nCONTENTS. infra/compose/docker-compose.yml: the three class slots on the review-bot service, with REBAR_LLM_MODEL deliberately absent. tests/unit/test_reviewbot_bedrock_cutover.py: a new hermetic test file (deliberately NOT added to tests/unit/test_review_bot.py — an agent in another session is concurrently adding ~350 lines to that file for bug 9ec0, and sharing it would have created a needless conflict).\n\nTHE DRIFT TEST HAS TEETH — MUTATION-VERIFIED, not asserted. Each of the three assertions is two-sided (exact class-SET equality plus byte-equal values), because a one-sided 'the compose value appears in the toml' check would pass while the toml grew an id the bot never uses. Five mutations, each applied alone against the committed tree and then reverted:\n\n    BASELINE                                            exit 0, 3 passed\n    compose STANDARD slot sonnet -> opus                exit 1  ('disagree for class ...')\n    bedrock.toml standard sonnet-4-6 -> sonnet-4-5      exit 1  (caught from the OTHER side)\n    add REBAR_LLM_MODEL to the service                  exit 1  ('sets REBAR_LLM_MODEL')\n    one alarm ModelId dimension changed                 exit 1  ('the alarm watches [...]')\n    delete the trivial class from bedrock.toml          exit 1  ('disagree on which classes exist')\n    RESTORED                                            exit 0, 3 passed, tree clean\n\nSo editing EITHER side alone fails, which is what the criterion asks for.\n\nA THIRD ASSERTION BEYOND WHAT THE CRITERION REQUIRED, and why. The alarm from Change 1 is dimensioned by ModelId because AWS/Bedrock is an AWS-published namespace and an alarm's dimensions cannot be wildcarded. So re-pointing the class slots WITHOUT updating the alarm leaves it watching ids that receive no traffic — it would read healthy forever, which is a silent monitoring failure rather than a loud one. The test therefore also pins the alarm's ModelId dimensions to the same three ids with the 'bedrock:' qualifier stripped, locating the alarm BY NAME across infra/terraform/*.tf rather than by filename so moving the resource does not break the test for an unrelated reason. Mutation D above is that assertion's teeth.\n\nPROCESS HONESTY — A MISTAKE I MADE AND CAUGHT. My first mutation round was INVALID and I am recording it rather than quietly re-running. I mutated and then restored with 'git checkout -- <file>' while my compose edit was still UNCOMMITTED, so the restore reverted to the parent commit and silently deleted the three class slots. Mutations A was clean but B, C and D then ran against a tree with NO slots at all, so their failures were confounded — two failures each, one of which was my own destroyed edit rather than the mutation. The tell was the RESTORED baseline reading '1 failed, 2 passed' instead of 3 passed. I re-applied the edit, COMMITTED it first, and re-ran all five against the committed tree; the table above is the valid round. The lesson generalises to this whole ticket: a mutation experiment is only valid if the restore returns to the state you think it does, and 'git checkout --' restores to HEAD, not to your working state.\n\nGATES ON CHANGE 2, by exit code, tree asserted clean (git status --porcelain empty) beforehand: make lint 0 (after fixing an I001 import-order finding that the pre-commit hook REJECTED the first commit for — the hook works), make typecheck 0, scripts/check_config_reads.py 0, scripts/gen_env_registry.py --check 0, terraform fmt -check -recursive 0. Full non-integration suite is running against 07d2ecfe54 and will be reported separately; nothing is pushed until it returns."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785797646024659001,"uuid":"43fa30ea-c5b0-41e9-9a7f-4cf72e7c294d"}